WEBVTT

1
00:00:09.647 --> 00:00:16.553
Time flew quickly and before long, Jo's second story, entitled "Beautiful Strangers," was published in the Eagle (the

2
00:00:16.593 --> 00:00:17.514
name of the newspaper).

3
00:00:18.214 --> 00:00:22.818
The editor of the paper liked Jo's stories so much that he bought four more of her works.

4
00:00:23.619 --> 00:00:28.904
"The ending is so tragic, Jo," Beth remarked, pulling her sweater tightly around herself.

5
00:00:29.864 --> 00:00:34.026
The sudden drop in temperatures had brought about a colder winter than anyone anticipated.

6
00:00:34.046 --> 00:00:40.149
"I felt this story should be more realistic; not so fanciful," the writer explained.

7
00:00:40.169 --> 00:00:46.112
"Well, I think you should have made it happier," criticized the artist, as she thumped a lump of

8
00:00:46.192 --> 00:00:47.532
clay on the table to soften it.

9
00:00:48.313 --> 00:00:54.416
Amy had moved on to experimenting with clay, and was molding small sculptures of everyone and everything these

10
00:00:54.476 --> 00:01:01.009
days. Mother's familiar footsteps coming up the stairs outside sent the girls scurrying along to welcome her home.

11
00:01:01.770 --> 00:01:07.131
Tossing the newspaper onto the sofa, Beth raced to the door as Meg plumped up the cushions on

12
00:01:07.171 --> 00:01:10.912
Mother's chair and Jo ran to warm her slippers by the fireplace.

13
00:01:11.792 --> 00:01:14.793
Amy wiped the clay muck off her hands and cleaned up her mess.

14
00:01:14.813 --> 00:01:17.094
"Hello, my girls!"

15
00:01:17.514 --> 00:01:19.695
Mother came into the room, holding out her arms.

16
00:01:20.395 --> 00:01:24.456
Her daughters embraced her, forming a circle so Mother would warm up quickly.

17
00:01:25.288 --> 00:01:30.673
"I'd better see to dinner," Meg said, leaving her sisters tending Mother while she went to help Hannah.

18
00:01:30.693 --> 00:01:33.135
"Do we have any letters from Father?"

19
00:01:33.475 --> 00:01:33.996
asked Beth.

20
00:01:34.916 --> 00:01:40.421
Only Beth remembered that it was letter day and Father always sent a message, whether it was brief

21
00:01:40.521 --> 00:01:41.802
or detailed, to his family.

22
00:01:42.663 --> 00:01:46.686
"I checked but there wasn't anything today," Mother said in a gentle voice.

23
00:01:47.547 --> 00:01:52.992
The girls grew silent, not wanting to ask questions that might result in answers they dreaded to hear.

24
00:01:53.980 --> 00:02:00.382
Sensing their uneasiness, Mother said, "It's not easy to write every week, especially when the soldiers are moving

25
00:02:00.462 --> 00:02:01.382
around so much.

26
00:02:01.962 --> 00:02:03.883
I'm sure your father is just fine."

27
00:02:04.963 --> 00:02:07.584
A sudden knock on the door interrupted their conversation.

28
00:02:08.564 --> 00:02:12.445
Puzzled, Mother got up and opened the door to find a young man in uniform shivering in the

29
00:02:12.485 --> 00:02:16.326
cold. He held a thin square envelope in his hand.

30
00:02:16.346 --> 00:02:19.027
"Mrs. March?

31
00:02:19.607 --> 00:02:22.068
Telegram for you," he said in an emotionless voice.

32
00:02:23.620 --> 00:02:28.802
Mother took the envelope with shaky hands but held onto Jo for support as she made her way

33
00:02:28.902 --> 00:02:29.723
back to her chair.

34
00:02:29.743 --> 00:02:31.823
"Jo, could you, please?

35
00:02:32.604 --> 00:02:36.946
I can't Mother, who was usually calm and cool, sounded frightened like a little girl.

36
00:02:38.146 --> 00:02:42.748
Impatiently ripping the envelope open, Jo read the message on the telegram, "Mrs.

37
00:02:42.828 --> 00:02:45.689
March, your husband is very ill.

38
00:02:46.550 --> 00:02:47.690
Must come at once."

39
00:02:48.570 --> 00:02:52.192
Jo stifled a cry as the telegram dropped from her hands, falling to the floor.

40
00:02:53.076 --> 00:02:57.458
Mother sat erect but silent tears fell onto her lap while she tried to comfort Beth and Amy,

41
00:02:57.859 --> 00:02:58.999
who were wailing loudly.

42
00:02:59.019 --> 00:03:01.341
"What's the matter?"

43
00:03:01.841 --> 00:03:04.662
Meg ran into the room, Hannah close at her heels.

44
00:03:05.443 --> 00:03:10.325
When Jo told them about the telegram, Meg collapsed onto the sofa, holding her head in her hands.

45
00:03:11.126 --> 00:03:13.247
Hannah cried, "Poor Mr.

46
00:03:13.347 --> 00:03:15.608
March. What are we to do?"

47
00:03:16.629 --> 00:03:21.412
The grieving period for Father was short since time was running out and Mother had to leave immediately

48
00:03:21.472 --> 00:03:21.992
to be with him.

49
00:03:23.496 --> 00:03:26.238
"Meg, pack extra socks and sweaters for Father.

50
00:03:27.379 --> 00:03:30.442
Beth, go downstairs and ask Hannah for my medicine bag."

51
00:03:31.323 --> 00:03:34.726
At that moment, the doorbell rang and Amy went to see who it was.

52
00:03:36.107 --> 00:03:39.650
When Mother and Meg came downstairs, they recognized the voices of Mr.

53
00:03:39.670 --> 00:03:41.111
Laurence, Laurie, and Mr.

54
00:03:41.131 --> 00:03:43.453
Brookes. "Mrs.

55
00:03:43.493 --> 00:03:46.475
March, young Amy just told us the terrible news.

56
00:03:47.356 --> 00:03:51.800
We are at your service to do whatever is needed in order to help you and your girls,"

57
00:03:52.756 --> 00:03:57.337
Mr. Laurence offered kindly, taking Mother's arm as he led her into the living room.

58
00:03:57.357 --> 00:03:59.218
"How did you know?"

59
00:03:59.598 --> 00:04:00.358
Meg asked Mr.

60
00:04:00.398 --> 00:04:02.979
Brookes, who also extended his arm to help her.

61
00:04:02.999 --> 00:04:09.721
"Laurie saw a soldier walking toward your house, and we all know that the army only sends a

62
00:04:09.761 --> 00:04:11.942
soldier if something is seriously wrong."

63
00:04:12.742 --> 00:04:17.263
Mother bowed her head in gratitude saying: "I am always grateful for your help, Mr.

64
00:04:17.283 --> 00:04:24.162
Laurence." Shaking his head, he replied, "Please don't mention it since it's not only our duty but also

65
00:04:24.402 --> 00:04:27.244
our honor to help friends in their time of need."

66
00:04:28.425 --> 00:04:31.546
"Would you allow me to go with you so that you're not traveling alone?"

67
00:04:31.907 --> 00:04:32.387
asked Mr.

68
00:04:32.407 --> 00:04:37.090
Brookes. Mother's eyes filled with tears as she nodded, accepting his gracious offer.

69
00:04:37.110 --> 00:04:39.071
"Now, Mrs.

70
00:04:39.131 --> 00:04:41.152
March, time is crucial," said Mr.

71
00:04:41.192 --> 00:04:46.155
Laurence firmly, "so I think you should prepare your things and leave as soon as possible."

72
00:04:46.175 --> 00:04:50.230
"Grandfather, shall I ask Jenson to get the carriage ready?"

73
00:04:50.610 --> 00:04:52.531
asked Laurie, as he made his way to the door.

74
00:04:52.551 --> 00:04:57.956
"Yes, and make sure Cook has prepared enough food and drink for the journey," Mr.

75
00:04:57.976 --> 00:05:01.679
Laurence called out as his grandson left to speak with the stable manager.

76
00:05:03.460 --> 00:05:05.241
Within an hour Mother was ready to leave.

77
00:05:05.922 --> 00:05:09.725
Doing a last-minute check, she looked around to see if she had everything she needed.

78
00:05:09.745 --> 00:05:11.326
"Where's Jo?"

79
00:05:11.646 --> 00:05:13.648
Mother asked, unable to see her second daughter.

80
00:05:14.672 --> 00:05:18.456
"She's somewhere, I'm sure," said Meg, helping Mother put her coat on.

81
00:05:18.476 --> 00:05:24.442
"Tell Father we love him," said Beth, trying with difficulty to control her tears.

82
00:05:24.462 --> 00:05:28.505
"We're praying for his rapid recovery," said Amy.

83
00:05:28.525 --> 00:05:34.331
"Girls, you must mind Meg and Jo, and don't forget the Hummels, who need our help as much

84
00:05:34.391 --> 00:05:35.752
as anyone," said Mother.

85
00:05:35.772 --> 00:05:39.316
"The carriage is ready," said Laurie, poking his head in the door.

86
00:05:40.092 --> 00:05:44.815
As Mother turned to say good-bye, Jo, shoving Laurie forcefully, suddenly burst in through the door.

87
00:05:45.635 --> 00:05:47.937
She was wearing a bonnet tied carelessly around her head.

88
00:05:47.957 --> 00:05:51.059
"I made it in time," she gasped.

89
00:05:51.619 --> 00:05:52.880
"Where have you been, Jo?"

90
00:05:53.140 --> 00:05:54.741
Meg asked in an annoyed voice.

91
00:05:54.761 --> 00:06:00.264
"To get something to contribute to Father's well-being," Jo answered in a trembling voice.

92
00:06:01.144 --> 00:06:06.668
She handed Mother some bills and continued, "It's not much, Mother, but I don't have anything else to

93
00:06:06.708 --> 00:06:11.679
give." Counting the money, Mother questioned her daughter, "This is a lot, Jo.

94
00:06:12.199 --> 00:06:13.280
Where did you get it from?"

95
00:06:14.341 --> 00:06:19.145
Untying her bonnet's ribbons, Jo let the bonnet slide off her head as she stood in silence.

96
00:06:19.165 --> 00:06:21.467
"Jo! Your hair!"

97
00:06:21.707 --> 00:06:22.368
screamed Amy.

98
00:06:22.388 --> 00:06:29.914
"I had no choice," explained Jo, running her fingers through the short, boyish cut, and continued, "I remember

99
00:06:29.934 --> 00:06:32.756
that the barber said he bought hair and used it to make wigs.

100
00:06:33.677 --> 00:06:38.365
So her voice broke, but she bravely carried on, "I sold it."

101
00:06:39.425 --> 00:06:44.829
Mother held Jo tightly in her arms and whispered, "Your father will know of your sacrifice and he

102
00:06:44.869 --> 00:06:46.190
will be so proud of you."

103
00:06:46.210 --> 00:06:53.815
"I'm sorry, but you must leave immediately," Laurie interrupted gently, deliberately avoiding Jo's eyes since he had not

104
00:06:53.875 --> 00:06:56.177
decided yet how he felt about her new haircut.

105
00:06:56.977 --> 00:07:00.960
Not wanting Mother to worry, the girls forced themselves to smile and look hopeful.

106
00:07:01.881 --> 00:07:06.704
As soon as Mother had closed the door behind her, however, the March sisters all broke down weeping.
